    {
      "claim_text": "Because a checkpoint can only reset the WAL when no other connection is using it, a checkpoint will always reset the WAL whenever the WAL is unused.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "While Passage [7] states that a checkpoint can only reset the WAL if there are no other connections using it, it does not guarantee that a checkpoint *will* always reset the WAL whenever the WAL is unused. Other factors (like the specific checkpoint type requested, e.g., PASSIVE vs FULL) determine if a reset occurs. A PASSIVE checkpoint might not reset even if unused if it hasn't reached the end of the WAL content to transfer, though typically it tries to complete. However, the text emphasizes that PASSIVE checkpoints 'might not run to completion'. The logical leap from 'can only reset if unused' to 'will always reset if unused' ignores the behavior of PASSIVE checkpoints which may stop early for other reasons or simply not be invoked. More critically, Passage [6] says 'In normal cases... a checkpoint is automatically run and the WAL file is recycled.' It doesn't make the universal guarantee claimed here for any checkpoint invocation.",
      "citations": []
