      "claim_text": "The automatic checkpoint runs in the same thread as the COMMIT that pushes the WAL over its size limit.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] states: 'By default, the checkpoint will be run automatically by the same thread that does the COMMIT that pushes the WAL over its size limit.'",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "By default, the checkpoint will be run automatically by the same thread that does the COMMIT that pushes the WAL over its size limit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Long-running readers never delay the automatic checkpoint.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [1] states that a checkpoint 'must stop when it reaches a page in the WAL that is past the end mark of any current reader.' Passage [2] explicitly says 'a long-running read transaction can prevent a checkpointer from making progress.' Passage [8] notes that if there is always at least one active reader, 'no checkpoints will be able to complete'. Therefore, long-running readers definitely delay or prevent the completion of checkpoints.",
      "citations": [
